course: dictionary
lesson: 3992
title: Gallop: fast running and fast doing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Gallop has three: the fast way a horse runs, a horse's fastest pace, and, informally, doing something very fast.
explain: «The horse's gallop soon became a trot, and the trot a steady walk.» Here a gallop is the fastest pace. The trot and the walk that follow are slower, so the gallop must have been the fastest of the three.
explain: «He unties the tow-rope, jumps on the horse and gallops off.» Gallops is a verb, telling you what he does. Try the swap check: he rides off very fast. That still makes sense, so gallops means rides or runs off at full speed.
explain: Informal use: «galloping prices» means prices rising very fast. Prices do not have legs, so the word is being used in a picture-word way. When a word seems odd for the thing it describes, look for the figurative meaning.
fact: To gallop means to run very fast, the way a horse does.
fact: A gallop is a horse's fastest pace. The four main paces, from slowest to fastest, are walk, trot, canter and gallop.
fact: In 1878 the photographer Eadweard Muybridge used a row of cameras to prove that a galloping horse has a moment with all four feet off the ground.
fact: In PE, children can gallop as a movement skill, with long, bouncing strides.
fact: Informally, «galloping prices» means prices are rising very fast.
exercise: «The horse's gallop soon became a trot.» In this sentence, what is the gallop? | A slow walk | A sleepy nap in the grass | *The fastest pace of the horse | A kind of saddle
exercise: Which list puts the paces in order, from slowest to fastest? | *walk, trot, canter, gallop | gallop, walk, canter, trot | canter, gallop, walk, trot | trot, walk, gallop, canter
exercise: Dad says, «Galloping prices are ruining the weekly shop.» What does galloping mean here? | Horses running in the field | Hidden under the table | Falling slowly | *Rising very fast
test: In 1878 Muybridge's photos proved a galloping horse has a moment when what happens? | Standing on one leg | Lying down to rest | Eating grass on the common | *All four feet are off the ground
test: Which word means a horse's fastest way of running? | trot | *gallop | walk | canter
exam: Ella says, «Gallop always means a horse racing.» Is she right? | No, it means a kind of sleep | *No, it can also mean running very fast or doing something very fast | Yes, it always means a horse racing | Yes, but only in films
